cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  Specialty Distribution and Surgeon-Level Concentration of Sling Surgery for
  Stress Urinary Incontinence in Fee-for-Service Medicare, 2013-2023
message: >-
  If you use this analysis pipeline or its derived outputs, please cite the
  manuscript once published. Until then, cite this repository.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Muffly
    given-names: Tyler
    name-suffix: MD
    email: tyler.muffly@dhha.org
repository-code: https://github.com/mufflyt/sling-volume-patterns
abstract: >-
  A reproducible R pipeline characterizing which physicians perform sling
  surgery for stress urinary incontinence (CPT 57288) in fee-for-service
  Medicare, how annual case volume is distributed across surgeons, and how both
  changed between 2013 and 2023. Physicians are classified from CMS provider
  type cross-referenced against the American Board of Obstetrics and Gynecology
  and American Board of Urology certification rosters, which splits
  Urogynecology and Reconstructive Pelvic Surgery by training pathway rather
  than collapsing it into a single group. Concentration is reported both within
  calendar year (primary) and pooled across years (secondary). Every number in
  the manuscript is computed from a frozen analysis cache at render time rather
  than typed in, so the prose cannot drift from the analysis.
keywords:
  - stress urinary incontinence
  - midurethral sling
  - urogynecology
  - Medicare
  - surgical workforce
  - procedural concentration
  - health services research
  - reproducible research
license: MIT
license-url: https://github.com/mufflyt/sling-volume-patterns/blob/main/LICENSE
version: 0.9.0
date-released: '2026-08-17'
references:
  - type: data
    title: >-
      Medicare Physician & Other Practitioners - by Provider and Service
    authors:
      - name: Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services
    url: >-
      https://data.cms.gov/provider-summary-by-type-of-service/medicare-physician-other-practitioners/medicare-physician-other-practitioners-by-provider-and-service
    notes: >-
      Annual releases 2013 through 2023. Cells covering fewer than 11
      beneficiaries are suppressed, so low-volume surgeons are systematically
      absent from the data.
